From c4cf440793e93ac74cae322950c7bde16ebdb9b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 00:50:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [meta] do not copy callee Old/Pre labels into \written and
 \read instances

When a \writing or \reading property is checked at a call to a function
whose footprint comes from its contract (an undefined callee, under the
default -meta-check-ext, or -meta-check-callee-assigns), inspect_contract
substitutes the formals of each assigns location (or \from location, for
\read) and of the assumes clauses by the actual arguments, and the result
becomes \written or \read in an assertion attached to the call.

The terms are copied with the labels of the contract. In an assigns
clause, a labelled logic function called without a label (strlen(s) in the
libc specification of strcpy, for instance) is typed with Old, and a user
may write \old(*p) or \at(*p, Pre). In the generated assertion:

- Old is not a valid label in a statement annotation. -check reports
  "Logic label Old is not declared in this scope", the kernel rejects the
  printed code ("logic label `Old' not found"), WP stops with
  Wp__NormAtLabels.LabelError and E-ACSL with "invalid label 'Old' within
  statement annotation 'Assertion'".
- Pre is valid but denotes the pre-state of the caller, not the state
  before the call, so the instance silently checks another location.

Both labels denote the pre-state of the callee, which is the state just
before the call. Map them to the meta label Before in the substitution
visitor: replace_after_before already turns it into Here when the
assertion precedes the call, and into a C label on the call when it is
placed after it (a property that uses After).

New test tests/meta/callee_labels.c covers an implicit Old, \old, \at(.,
Pre) and an After property for \written, and Old and Pre in \from parts
and assumes clauses for \read, in the default and fold configurations.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
